# Blueprint-coverage questions for CCAR-P (official domains that were thin or missing).
# (category, difficulty, question, correct, [wrong x3], explanation)
SD='solution-design-and-architecture'; MP='claude-models-prompting-and-context'
IN='integration'; EV='evaluation-testing-and-optimization'
GV='governance-safety-and-risk-management'; ST='stakeholder-communication-and-lifecycle-management'
DP='developer-productivity-and-operational-management'
Q = [
(SD,'hard',"An insurer wants an assistant that answers adjusters' questions about a single 80,000-token policy manual that changes once a quarter. Traffic is about 2,000 questions a day and most are simple lookups. Which starting design is most defensible?",
 "Place the manual in a stable, cached prompt prefix and ask each question after it; add retrieval only if the corpus outgrows the context window or evaluations show accuracy problems",
 ["Build a vector index with chunking, hybrid search and a reranker before testing anything simpler","Fine-tune a model on the manual and retire the prompt","Use an orchestrator with several specialist subagents, one per manual chapter"],
 "The corpus fits the context window and is stable, which is the shape prompt caching rewards. Move to retrieval or agents only when a measured limit demands it; the simplest sufficient structure wins."),
(SD,'medium',"A sponsor says: 'Use Claude to cut the time analysts spend on quarterly reports.' What should the architect do before choosing a pattern or model?",
 "Translate the goal into measurable outcomes (baseline hours per report, accuracy tolerance, turnaround, volume) and map the inputs, processing, outputs and feedback loop",
 ["Pick the largest model so quality cannot be the constraint","Start with a multi-agent design to leave room for growth","Choose the vector database and embedding model first"],
 "Architecture follows measurable requirements. Without a baseline and targets there is nothing to design against or to evaluate later."),
(SD,'medium',"A deployed assistant collects thumbs-down ratings, but nothing changes after release. Which feedback design closes the loop?",
 "Log each rating with a trace ID, prompt version and retrieved context, triage failures into labelled categories, and feed them into the evaluation set and prompt or retrieval changes",
 ["Append every disliked answer to the system prompt as a negative example","Retrain the model automatically on thumbs-up conversations","Review ratings only if the average score falls below a threshold"],
 "Feedback is useful when it is traceable and categorised so it can drive evaluations and targeted fixes. Auto-editing prompts or retraining on raw ratings is uncontrolled."),
(MP,'medium',"A team picks the largest Claude model for a document-extraction feature because 'accuracy matters'. What is the better selection method?",
 "Run a representative golden set across model tiers and choose the cheapest tier that meets the accuracy threshold within the latency budget, routing only hard cases to a stronger tier",
 ["Keep the largest model, since any cheaper tier will be less accurate","Choose the fastest tier and accept the quality that results","Choose by published benchmark rankings without testing on your documents"],
 "Model choice is a measured trade-off of quality, latency and cost on your own task, not a default to the top tier."),
(MP,'medium',"A high-volume ticket-classification job runs with extended thinking at a large budget. Evaluations show accuracy no better than without thinking, while latency and cost are much higher. What should the team do?",
 "Disable or reduce thinking and effort for this workload, confirm on the evaluation set, and reserve deeper reasoning for the hard-reasoning route",
 ["Raise the thinking budget further until accuracy improves","Keep the setting because reasoning can only help","Switch to batch processing and leave thinking unchanged"],
 "Reasoning tokens are billed as output and add latency. Spend them only where measured quality gains justify it."),
(IN,'hard',"A retailer's platform and security policy are standardised on Google Cloud, with data-residency commitments in one region. It wants Claude with minimal new infrastructure to own. What should the architect do first?",
 "Evaluate Claude on Vertex AI, confirming model availability in the required region, the features the design needs, IAM integration and data-handling terms before committing",
 ["Call the first-party API from a VM in that region and assume the same model IDs and features apply","Assume Vertex AI model identifiers and features match the first-party API exactly","Self-host an open-weights model to avoid platform differences"],
 "The deployment surface is an architecture decision. Existing Google Cloud governance points to Vertex AI, but model IDs, regions and feature availability differ by platform, so verify before committing."),
(IN,'medium',"An enterprise is standardised on Microsoft Azure with Entra ID for access control and wants to use Claude. Which first step is most appropriate?",
 "Evaluate the Azure-hosted offering for Claude (Microsoft Foundry), confirming regional model availability, required features, Entra-based access control and data terms",
 ["Use the first-party API directly and ignore the existing Azure governance","Assume every Claude feature is available on Azure with identical model IDs","Build a custom proxy that stores shared API keys in application code"],
 "Existing cloud governance is a strong signal for the deployment surface, but availability and features must be verified per platform."),
(IN,'hard',"The same application must run on Amazon Bedrock in one region and on Vertex AI in another. Which design best limits risk?",
 "Isolate platform-specific model IDs, authentication and feature flags behind an adapter, and run contract tests against each platform",
 ["Hard-code first-party model IDs and assume the platforms are identical","Fork the codebase per platform and let each drift","Use the lowest common feature set permanently without checking what each platform offers"],
 "Model identifiers, authentication and feature availability differ by platform. An adapter plus per-platform contract tests contains that variation."),
(IN,'hard',"A RAG system over legal contracts uses fixed 500-token windows. Answers often cite the wrong clause because clauses are split mid-sentence. What is the best fix?",
 "Chunk along document structure (clauses and sections), keep section identifiers and headings with each chunk as context, and re-evaluate retrieval",
 ["Increase the chunk size to 5,000 tokens","Lower the temperature so the model cites more carefully","Switch to a larger embedding model without changing the chunks"],
 "Chunking should follow document structure and query patterns. Arbitrary windows break the units that answers depend on, and no downstream setting repairs that."),
(IN,'hard',"Hybrid retrieval often returns the relevant chunk at ranks 7 to 12, but only the top three chunks are passed to the model, so answers miss key facts. Which change is most appropriate?",
 "Retrieve a wider candidate set, rerank it with a reranker or model-based scorer, then pass only the best few chunks",
 ["Pass all candidates to the model without reranking","Raise the temperature so the model explores more","Shrink every chunk so more fit in the top three"],
 "Recall and precision are separate problems: retrieve broadly, then rerank to put the best evidence in the limited context."),
(IN,'medium',"Agents built by different teams on different frameworks must discover each other's capabilities and delegate tasks, while each agent also needs access to its own databases and APIs. Which pairing fits?",
 "MCP to connect each agent to its tools and data, and an agent-to-agent protocol for discovery and task delegation between independently owned agents",
 ["MCP for everything, wrapping each agent as a database query tool","A shared system prompt listing every agent's instructions","Command-line tools only, invoked through a shell"],
 "MCP standardises tool and data access; agent-to-agent protocols address communication between autonomous agents. They solve different integration problems."),
(IN,'medium',"A coding agent needs git, jq and the project's test runner. A teammate proposes building an MCP server wrapping each command. What is the better judgment?",
 "Use the existing command-line tools through a shell tool, and reserve MCP for capabilities needing typed access, an authorisation boundary or reuse across clients",
 ["Wrap every command in MCP so all tools look uniform","Avoid command-line tools because models cannot use them reliably","Write one MCP tool per command flag for maximum control"],
 "Familiar CLIs are cheap and effective for local work. MCP earns its cost where structure, security boundaries or sharing across clients matter."),
(IN,'hard',"An agent exposes 120 tools. Loading every definition costs about 40,000 tokens per request and tool-selection errors are rising. Which approach is best?",
 "Expose a small core set and discover the rest on demand through tool search or deferred loading, so definitions enter context only when needed",
 ["Keep all definitions and ask the model to choose carefully","Concatenate shorter one-line descriptions for all 120 tools","Raise max_tokens so the definitions fit more comfortably"],
 "Large tool catalogues dilute context and hurt selection. Progressive discovery keeps the working set small."),
(EV,'hard',"A new prompt is A/B tested on 10% of traffic. After one day and 40 sessions the thumbs-up rate is higher and latency is unchanged. A manager wants to ship. What is the right response?",
 "Do not ship yet: fix the primary metric and guardrails in advance, randomise assignment, run until the sample gives adequate power, and check segment results before promoting",
 ["Ship, because the metric moved in the right direction","Double the traffic share and decide after one more day","Ignore thumbs-up and decide on the average response length"],
 "Early, small samples are noisy. A valid test fixes metrics and guardrails up front, randomises exposure and runs long enough to separate signal from variance."),
(EV,'hard',"A RAG assistant gives wrong answers. How should the team localise the cause?",
 "Evaluate retrieval separately (was the gold passage retrieved, recall at k) from generation (is the answer faithful to the retrieved context)",
 ["Replace the model with a larger one and re-test","Increase k to 50 and judge by overall satisfaction","Lower the temperature and re-run the same tests"],
 "A RAG failure is either missing evidence or misuse of it. Separate metrics tell you which stage to fix."),
(EV,'medium',"Before launching a support assistant, the team must build an evaluation set. Which approach is strongest?",
 "Sample representative, anonymised queries stratified by intent and risk, add edge and adversarial cases with labelled expected outcomes, and keep a holdout not used for tuning",
 ["Write twenty easy questions the team already knows the assistant answers","Use only synthetic queries generated by the same prompt under test","Wait for production traffic and evaluate from complaints"],
 "A useful evaluation set is representative, labelled, covers risk and edge cases, and stays separate from tuning data."),
(EV,'medium',"Monthly Claude spend doubled with unchanged traffic, but logs only record daily totals. What should the team add first?",
 "Record per-request input, output and cache read and write tokens, tagged by workload and prompt version, to find the cost driver",
 ["Switch every workload to the smallest model","Cap max_tokens globally and hope cost falls","Disable prompt caching to simplify billing"],
 "You cannot optimise what you cannot attribute. Per-request usage by workload and version locates the regression before any change is made."),
(GV,'hard',"An EU customer-support assistant sends tickets containing names and emails to Claude and stores transcripts in a vector index. A user requests erasure. Which design gap does this expose?",
 "No data map or deletion path covering transcripts, logs and the vector index, and no minimisation, retention limits or processor agreement defined up front",
 ["The model weights must be retrained to forget the user","A system-prompt line telling the model to forget is missing","Nothing, because the provider handles erasure for every downstream store"],
 "GDPR duties (minimisation, retention, erasure, processor terms) must be designed into every store the application controls, including logs and indexes."),
(GV,'hard',"A federal agency requires FedRAMP-authorised processing. The team plans to use the newest beta feature on the first-party API. What should the architect do first?",
 "Confirm that the chosen deployment surface, region and specific features fall inside an authorised boundary at the required level, and drop any that do not",
 ["Assume any enterprise plan satisfies the requirement","Encrypt prompts client-side and proceed with the beta feature","Treat authorisation as a late paperwork step after the build"],
 "Compliance attaches to a specific service boundary and feature set, so it constrains architecture from the start."),
(GV,'medium',"A lending assistant drafts decisions that affect applicants. Which human-oversight design is most appropriate?",
 "Route by risk and uncertainty: high-impact decisions always get human approval, low-grounding or policy-gap cases are flagged, and a sample of the rest is audited",
 ["Review a random 5% of all outputs and automate the rest","Review nothing because the model is highly accurate in testing","Have the model approve its own decisions with a second prompt"],
 "Oversight should follow impact and uncertainty, not random sampling alone, and important decisions need approval."),
(ST,'medium',"A sponsor asks for 'an AI assistant for HR'. Which discovery output matters most before architecture starts?",
 "Agreed use cases, users, data sources and their sensitivity, success metrics with a baseline, constraints (latency, cost, compliance) and key risks",
 ["A benchmark comparison of available models","High-fidelity UI mockups","A vendor price list"],
 "Discovery turns a vague request into agreed requirements and constraints that the architecture can be judged against."),
(ST,'medium',"Six months after launch a new team asks why the solution uses retrieval rather than fine-tuning. Which practice would have answered this?",
 "Architecture decision records capturing context, options considered, trade-offs, the decision and its consequences",
 ["Searching old chat threads for the discussion","Comments in the code explaining each function","A verbal handoff at the end of the project"],
 "Decision records preserve the reasoning behind a design, which supports handoff and future change."),
(ST,'hard',"An executive demands sub-second latency, the highest accuracy and the lowest cost. How should the architect respond?",
 "Present measured options as trade-offs (for example tiered routing), ask the sponsor to rank priorities, and agree written targets",
 ["Promise all three to keep the project moving","Choose silently and report the result afterwards","Decline until the requirements are consistent"],
 "Competing goals are resolved by showing evidence-backed options and agreeing priorities, not by overpromising or deciding alone."),
(DP,'hard',"An organisation wants to give 300 engineers a vetted set of Claude Code skills, agents, hooks and MCP servers, and enforce security settings developers cannot override. Which approach fits?",
 "Use managed settings for non-overridable enforcement and an organisation-controlled plugin marketplace to distribute the approved extensions",
 ["Publish the recommended setup on an internal wiki and ask teams to follow it","Commit settings in each repository and let teams edit them","Let each engineer install tools and audit machines quarterly"],
 "Enforcement and distribution are separate needs. Managed settings sit above lower scopes, and a managed marketplace gives consistent, controlled tooling."),
(DP,'medium',"A team wants to use Claude to help investigate a production incident. Which workflow is most appropriate?",
 "Give it read-only access to logs and traces, have it propose a fix as a pull request, and require human review before anything reaches production",
 ["Give it write access to production so it can apply fixes immediately","Paste secrets into the prompt so it can query systems directly","Disable all review to shorten time to resolution"],
 "Assistive debugging works best with least-privilege access and human review before changes are applied."),
]
